3. Dummy Node Pattern
Very important.
Whenever the head node might change, use a dummy node.
Problems
Remove Elements (LC 203)
Merge Two Sorted Lists (LC 21)
Swap Nodes in Pairs (LC 24)
Remove Duplicates

4. Reversal Pattern
One of the most important patterns.
Problems
Reverse Linked List (LC 206)
Reverse Linked List II (LC 92)
Palindrome Linked List
Reverse Nodes in K Group (LC 25)

5. Merge Pattern
Used when dealing with multiple sorted lists.
Problems
Merge Two Sorted Lists (LC 21)
Merge K Sorted Lists (LC 23)
Sort List (LC 148)
Idea
Compare nodes from two lists and attach smaller one

6. Divide & Conquer Pattern
Frequently mixed with Merge Pattern.
Problems
Sort List (LC 148)
Merge K Sorted Lists (LC 23)
Idea
Split list into halves.
Solve recursively.
Merge.
Uses Fast-Slow pointer just for splitting

7. Recursion Pattern
Problems
Reverse Linked List
Swap Nodes in Pairs
Merge Sorted Lists

8. Hashing Pattern
Used when extra space is allowed.
Problems
Linked List Cycle
Remove Duplicates
Intersection of Linked Lists

Reality of Interviews
For Linked Lists, around 90% of interview questions can be solved using combinations of:
✅ Fast & Slow Pointer
 ✅ Two Pointers
 ✅ Dummy Node
 ✅ Reversal
 ✅ Merge

If you master these 5 patterns,
you'll be able to solve almost every Linked
List problem asked in coding interviews and most LeetCode medium-level questions.
 As you're already studying patterns in DSA,
 after Linked Lists I'd recommend moving to:
 Sliding Window → Two Pointers (Array) → Prefix Sum → Binary Search → Backtracking → Heap → Graphs.
 This is a very interview-friendly progression.